Translate the following to assembly. Assume a 4-byte instruction size and a byte-addressed 
machine.                                                                                                      [4 Marks]  
int i; 
for (i=0; i<1024; i++) { 
A[i] = B[i] + C[i];}

i. Identify and explain the addressing mode used in the following code with a dia-
gram.lw $t1,8($t0)

ii. Translate the code snippet in C to MIPS assembly language.
While(1)
{ if(a == b)
{ temp = A[k];
A[k] = A[K+1];
A[k+1] = temp;
}
}
Given that $4 has the base address of the array A, and a, b, k are mapped to
registers $1, $2, $5 respectively.

Convert the following C statements to its equivalent MIPS assembly language 
statements.  Assume the variables i, j and k are associated with the registers $s1, $s2 
and $s3 respectively.                                                                                 

if ( i == j && i == k )  
    i++ ;  
else  
    j-- ;  
j = i + k ;

Write the MIPS code for the following C code:  


     ( 4 Marks )  [BTL-3] 
main() 
{  
     int s = 0; 
     while( I <= 5) 
        s = s + 1; 
     printf(“Sum is %d”, s); 
}
